{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Cooperstown's steam-railroad passenger link: the Cooperstown & Susquehanna Valley Railroad, chartered 1865 and opened July 14, 1869, ran 16 miles down the Susquehanna from Cooperstown to Colliersville, where it met the Albany & Susquehanna (later Delaware & Hudson) main line at a station named Cooperstown Junction. It was built to 6-foot broad gauge to match the A&S and was narrowed to standard gauge on May 28, 1876. A successor, the Cooperstown & Charlotte Valley Railroad (incorporated 1888), built a second line out of Cooperstown Junction: it ran SOUTH-WEST down the Susquehanna for about 3.5 miles, swung east at the mouth of Charlotte Creek, and followed the Charlotte Valley to West Davenport (1889) and Davenport Center (February 1890), aiming ultimately at Cooksburg and Catskill Creek; the D&H took stock control in 1903 and ran the line as its Cooperstown Branch, building the stone station that still stands. The last scheduled passenger train left Cooperstown on June 24, 1934. Freight lasted until the D&H moved to abandon the branch in 1971; Delaware Otsego Corporation bought it and revived the C&CV name, and since 1996 the Leatherstocking Chapter of the NRHS has run seasonal excursions over it between Cooperstown and Milford. Station order used here: Cooperstown - Phoenix Mills - Hyde Park - Milford - Portlandville - Colliersville (Cooperstown Junction). The Charlotte Valley line was the shorter-lived of the two: after the 1891 consolidation the Ulster & Delaware completed the connection east of West Davenport, and passenger service between Cooperstown Junction and the U&D connection ceased on August 10, 1905.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooperstown_and_Charlotte_Valley_Railroad (C&SV chartered 1865; construction began February 1868; first train July 14, 1869; 6-foot broad gauge narrowed to standard gauge May 28, 1876; C&CV incorporated 1888 for a line toward Davenport; D&H took stock control June 1903; passenger service ended June 24, 1934; D&H sold the 16-mile Cooperstown Junction-Cooperstown branch to Delaware Otsego Corporation in 1971; Leatherstocking Chapter NRHS heritage operation since 1996)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooperstown_and_Charlotte_Valley_Railroad (Charlotte Valley extension: 6 miles completed to West Davenport by 1889; 'In early February 1890, the C&CV tracklayers reached Davenport Center'; route planned on through Davenport Center and Harpersfield to Cooksburg; after the 1891 consolidation the Ulster & Delaware completed the connection, reaching Davenport Center by 1900; 'On August 10, 1905, passenger service between Cooperstown Junction and the U&D connection ceased')", "https://www.lrhs.com/history-of-the-cooperstown-and-charlotte-valley/ (Leatherstocking Railway Historical Society history page: 'the entire 16 miles was narrowed up'; last scheduled passenger train left Cooperstown June 24, 1934; D&H abandonment decision 1971)", "https://abandonedonline.net/location/cooperstown-charlotte-valley-railroad/"], "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API): ways named 'Cooperstown & Charlotte Valley Railroad' (railway=rail, operator=Leatherstocking Railway Historical Society, old_railway_operator=D&H, plus the abandoned/disused stubs at each end), routed Colliersville-Cooperstown by Dijkstra shortest path. The whole branch is real-traced with no straight-line approximation; the traced length is 25.7 km / 16.0 miles, matching the historical 16-mile figure.", "Cross-checked against the Wikimedia Commons map data behind the Delaware and Hudson Railway article's system map (Data:Cooperstown and Charlotte Valley Railroad.map, CC-BY-4.0), which is itself OSM-derived. The Cooperstown branch traced here agrees with it to a median of 7.8 m (p90 102 m, max 319 m), and the Colliersville-Cooperstown distance measured along the Commons line is 25.77 km against 25.75 km here - both exactly 16.0 miles. Note that the Commons map labels its 19.31-mile red line 'Cooperstown and Susquehanna Valley Railroad', but 3.3 miles of that is the Charlotte Valley line running south-west out of Cooperstown Junction, which is carried here as a separate feature with its own dates.", "Charlotte Valley line: OpenStreetMap ways named 'Davenport Branch' (railway=abandoned, old_railway_operator=D&H) plus the rail-trail segment through Robert V. Riddell State Park that carries railway:name=Davenport Branch, routed Cooperstown Junction - West Davenport by Dijkstra shortest path. 12.6 km / 7.8 miles, 100% real-traced with no straight-line approximation, agreeing with the Commons map to a median of 0.0 m. The trace STOPS at West Davenport: east of there the alignment on to Davenport Center was completed and worked by the Ulster & Delaware, and is a different railroad (see ulster_delaware_railroad.geojson)."], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact timetables for any era; frequencies below are estimated. The Charlotte Valley line's passenger-service START year is inferred from the construction dates (track reached West Davenport in 1889 and Davenport Center in February 1890), not from a sourced first-timetable date; only its end date, August 10, 1905, is directly sourced. The two short abandoned fragments OSM labels 'Cooperstown and Northern' north of the village are still omitted for lack of a reliable source on what they were.", "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Cooperstown and Susquehanna Valley Railroad", "line": "Cooperstown and Susquehanna Valley: Cooperstown \u2013 Cooperstown Junction", "id": "cooperstown-susquehanna-valley-rr", "color": "#2a6f4e", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: 16-mile mixed branch-line service, broad-gauge steam from 1869 and standard gauge from 1876, over a river-valley alignment with six stops; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1869, "end": 1875, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Opened July 14, 1869 as the Cooperstown & Susquehanna Valley Railroad, built to 6-foot broad gauge so it could interchange with the Albany & Susquehanna at Colliersville (Cooperstown Junction). Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooperstown_and_Charlotte_Valley_Railroad", "https://www.lrhs.com/history-of-the-cooperstown-and-charlotte-valley/", "https://abandonedonline.net/location/cooperstown-charlotte-valley-railroad/"]}, {"start": 1876, "end": 1902, "headway_min": 300, "note": "Narrowed to standard gauge on May 28, 1876, ending the break of gauge with the connecting main line. The Cooperstown & Charlotte Valley Railroad was incorporated in 1888 to extend the system toward Davenport. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cooperstown_and_Charlotte_Valley_Railroad", "https://www.lrhs.com/history-of-the-cooperstown-and-charlotte-valley/", "https://abandonedonline.net/location/cooperstown-charlotte-valley-railroad/"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordi